{"kind": "journal", "text": "Final selection. The dominant effect in this panel is a two part slow factor: (1) a volatility term structure tilt, sub(ts_z(std(60),250), ts_z(std(250),250)), favouring names whose recent 60 day vol is elevated relative to their own long run 250 day vol, and (2) skip recent month long horizon reversal, delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250),250)),20) and delay(neg(ts_z(ret(250),250)),20). The skip month structure was discovered, not assumed: the 52 week high reversal signal scores 1.25 undelayed but 1.53 at delay 15, 1.61 at delay 20 and 1.36 at delay 40, a smooth hump peaking at one month, which matches the textbook convention of skipping the most recent month so that short term reversal does not contaminate a long horizon signal. Evidence the family is real rather than fitted: four independent 250 day features agree in direction and magnitude, every weighting scheme I tried lands within a narrow band, the delay profile of the leading composite is a broad plateau (2.00 at lag 0, 2.17 at lag 5, 2.06 at lag 10) rather than a spike, and the composite still earns 1.28 to 1.33 even at a 40 day lag. I deliberately REJECTED two higher scoring options. First, adding neg(ret(5)) lifted the three factor composite from 2.03 to 2.14, but short term reversal flips sign from +0.90 to -0.40 under only a 5 day delay, so it is acutely lag sensitive and by far the highest turnover component; gross of costs it flatters the train SR while being the least likely piece to survive out of sample. Second, I avoided the very highest single train numbers where they sat on sharp peaks rather than plateaus. The three reported expressions deliberately span the one genuinely free hyperparameter, the implementation lag: an equal weight three factor version with no extra lag as the safety net, a lagged two factor version, and an ensemble of the two that hedges the lag choice and scored highest at 2.21.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:33:22.846686+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["add(cs_z(sub(ts_z(std(60), 250), ts_z(std(250), 250))), add(cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250), 250)), 20)), cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(ret(250), 250)), 20))))", "delay(add(cs_z(sub(ts_z(std(60), 250), ts_z(std(250), 250))), cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250), 250)), 20))), 5)", "add(cs_z(delay(add(cs_z(sub(ts_z(std(60), 250), ts_z(std(250), 250))), cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250), 250)), 20))), 5)), cs_z(add(cs_z(sub(ts_z(std(60), 250), ts_z(std(250), 250))), add(cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250), 250)), 20)), cs_z(delay(neg(ts_z(ret(250), 250)), 20))))))"], "chosen_sr_train": [2.028, 2.1744, 2.2123], "rationale": "All three are equal weight cs_z composites of one dominant slow factor: a volatility term structure tilt sub(ts_z(std(60),250), ts_z(std(250),250)) combined with skip recent month long horizon reversal, delay(neg(ts_z(maxr(250),250)),20) and delay(neg(ts_z(ret(250),250)),20). Train SR 2.03, 2.17 and 2.21. Chosen for persistence rather than peak train SR. Supporting evidence: four independent 250 day features (maxr, ret, ma, minr) agree in direction and magnitude under ts_z(.,250) normalization, so this is a horizon effect not a lucky expression; every weighting scheme tested lands in a narrow band, a flat optimum rather than a knife edge; and the delay profile is a broad plateau, 2.00 at lag 0, 2.17 at lag 5, 2.06 at lag 10, with the composite still earning about 1.3 even at a 40 day lag, confirming a genuinely slow low turnover factor. The one month skip was discovered empirically, the 52 week high reversal signal rises from 1.25 undelayed to 1.61 at a 20 day delay, and it matches the standard convention of skipping the recent month so short term reversal does not contaminate a long horizon signal. I excluded neg(ret(5)) despite it lifting train SR from 2.03 to 2.14, because it flips sign from +0.90 to -0.40 under a mere 5 day delay, marking it as lag sensitive, high turnover and unlikely to survive out of sample. The three reported variants span the only real free parameter, the implementation lag: an unlagged three factor safety net, a lagged two factor version, and an ensemble hedging the lag choice.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:33:34.266113+00:00"}
